<!--
    Application-wide look. Merged from App.xaml rather than the window, because
    a dozen dialogs are built in code as bare Windows and inherit application
    resources only — styling the window would leave them on the stock 2006 look.

    Everything here is presentation. No style sets Margin, Width, Height or
    alignment, so nothing moves: spacing still comes from the Padding and Margin
    the layout already specifies. Sizes that were tuned by hand stay tuned.

    ComboBox is retemplated, popup and all. It kept the stock template while the
    app was light-only; the stock one draws its own chrome from system brushes,
    so it stayed light when the palette went dark.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Shell;assembly=PresentationFramework">

    <!-- The palette lives in its own dictionary, merged ahead of this one by
         App.xaml. Every style below names colours only through those keys, so
         swapping light for dark is one line there and nothing here changes.

         Those references are DynamicResource, not Static: a static one is
         resolved once when the style loads and keeps the brush it found, so
         swapping the palette at runtime would leave every existing control
         wearing the old theme. Dynamic costs a lookup per use and buys a toggle
         that works without restarting. -->

    <!-- ─────────────── Brand ───────────────
         The mark as vector geometry rather than a bitmap, so it stays crisp
         at any DPI and takes its colour from the palette rather than the fill
         baked into the artwork — which is what lets it invert for a dark bar.

         Converted from erp.svg: WPF's geometry mini-language accepts SVG path
         data unchanged, and the F1 prefix selects the nonzero fill rule SVG
         uses, where WPF would otherwise default to even-odd and punch out the
         enclosed areas of letters like o and e. The artwork is 14 separate
         paths, which concatenate into one geometry as subpaths — no transforms,
         fills or clipping in the source to fold in first.

         Original viewBox 111.92 x 39.34, against Studio's 138.82 x 36.5. Both
         are two-line wordmarks sized by height, so height is what decides how
         big they read — the widths differ and always will. Studio sits at a
         Viewbox height of 28; this mark is set to 30 in MainWindow.xaml, which
         reads as the same weight against its squarer artwork. -->
